.Dd November 24, 2026
.Dt GB10SMMU 4
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm gb10smmu
.Nd SMMU in front of the GB10 GPU (kernel module gb10smmu.ko)
.Sh SYNOPSIS
To load the module at boot time, place the following lines in
.Xr loader.conf 5 :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
gb10smmu_load="YES"
gb10smmu.mode="2"
.Ed
.Pp
The mode is required: without it the module refuses to load.
The loader loads the module before any other module of the package, so the
GPU finds the SMMU answering while its firmware starts.
.Pp
Or load it by hand, with the mode set first:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
kenv gb10smmu.mode=2
kldload /boot/modules/gb10smmu.ko
.Ed
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
module programs the SMMUv3 that stands in front of the GPU of an NVIDIA
GB10 (the DGX Spark).
.Fx
leaves that SMMU switched off, in global bypass, so every request
the GPU makes to it is ignored.
.Pp
The GB10 boot ROM translates the address of the GSP firmware image with PCIe
ATS before it reads it.
With nothing answering, the SEC2 chain-of-trust command fails with the
undocumented error
.Ql 0x6
and the GPU firmware never boots.
.Nm
answers those requests: it builds a stream table in which the stream IDs of
the GPU use stage-2 translation and every other stream stays in bypass.
.Pp
The module is native
.Fx :
it uses no Linux interface and no Linux
source.
It is a bring-up aid, not an IOMMU driver.
It gives no DMA isolation, it knows the one SMMU of the one machine it runs
on, and it programs the SMMU by hand.
The proper fix is an IOMMU in the kernel: a device attachment that goes
through it, with IORT RMR support.
.Pp
The module finds the GB10 in the device tree of the running machine before
it touches anything, and it programs the SMMU that stands in front of that
GPU and of no other SMMU.
A machine without the GPU has nothing for the module to program: it refuses
to load and says so.
The SMMU it programs is the one the machine's own description names, never
one that is hardcoded in the module.
.Pp
The module is built and installed on arm64 only, and the loader is told to
load it only on a machine that has the GB10 GPU.
.Sh THE FACTS IT READS
Every address and every ID the module programs comes from the machine
itself, from the ACPI IORT table, which the firmware writes and
.Fx
exposes through
.Xr acpi 4 .
The module reads three things from it:
.Bl -tag -width Ds -offset indent
.It SMMUv3 node
the node of the GPU:
its base address, the register block the module maps and programs;
.It ID mapping
the mappings that give the PCI requester IDs of the GPU the stream IDs of
the SMMU, which is how the module learns which stream table entries need
stage-2 translation and which stay in bypass.
The GPU may have more than one such mapping, and the module installs all
of them;
.It RMR nodes
the memory ranges the firmware reserves for the GPU, which the trap mode
maps and every other access terminates.
.El
.Pp
The table is read by a parser that bounds every read against the length of
the table and against the length of the node the read belongs to, and that
gives up on anything it cannot make sense of.
There is no best effort: when the table does not add up, the module
refuses to load and says which node and which offset are at fault.
The same goes for a table that describes no SMMU for the GPU, or that maps
no requester ID to one, or that holds fewer nodes than it claims, or more
than it claims.
It does not fall back to an address of another board.
.Pp
The addresses the first version of the module used are kept only as a
cross-check: when the IORT says something else, the module says so and
programs what the IORT says.
.Pp
One thing does not come from the table: the PCI identity of the GPU.
The module reads it from the device tree of the running machine, and it
programs nothing when the machine has no such GPU.
.Sh MODES
The mode is chosen with the
.Ev gb10smmu.mode
loader variable, before the module loads.
It is required.
.Bl -tag -width Ds -offset indent
.It Va 1
.Ql trap :
the stage-2 table maps only the RMR regions the IORT reserves for the GPU.
Every other access is terminated and recorded in the event queue, so the
module can show what the GPU asks for.
The stage-2 table maps in blocks of 2 MiB, so a reserved region has to be
a whole number of 2 MiB blocks; a region that is not one cannot be mapped
without also mapping memory the firmware did not reserve, and the module
refuses to load and says which region it is.
.It Va 2
.Ql identity :
the stage-2 table identity-maps the first 512 GiB in 1 GiB blocks.
This is the mode the GB10 needs.
.El
.Pp
Three more loader variables:
.Bl -tag -width Ds -offset indent
.It Va gb10smmu.rmr
Whether the trap mode maps the RMR regions at all.
It is on by default;
set it to 0 to map nothing and see every access.
.It Va gb10smmu.eats
Whether the stage-2 stream table entries allow ATS translation requests:
0 refuses them, 1 allows them, 2 allows them with PASID.
It is 1 by default.
.It Va gb10smmu.seg
.It Va gb10smmu.rid
The PCI segment number and the PCI requester ID of the GPU.
They say which node of the IORT is the GPU's.
They default to the ones the GPU has in the device tree of the running
machine, which is where a single-segment machine has it.
Set them when the machine's firmware describes the GPU somewhere else.
.El
.Sh LOAD ORDER
.Nm
must be loaded before the nvidia(4) driver.
The GPU translates its addresses while its firmware starts, which is before
the driver opens the device, so the SMMU has to be answering by then.
.Pp
The module refuses to load while the nvidia(4) driver is attached to a
device, and it says so.
.Pp
The check for the driver and the change of the SMMU are one thing: they
happen under the same holds, the module's own lock and the bus-level
hold, so a driver that attaches at that moment waits for the module
instead of finding the SMMU half programmed.
.Pp
Never unload
.Nm
while the nvidia(4) driver
is loaded.
Unloading puts the SMMU back into global bypass, and a GPU that runs with a
translation table it can no longer answer faults.
The module refuses to unload when the driver is still attached, and when it
cannot put the SMMU back into bypass.
A refused unload leaves the module loaded: the kernel does not unload a
module whose unload handler returns an error.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds -offset indent
.It Pa /boot/modules/gb10smmu.ko
The module.
.El
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
The module has three nodes under
.Pa debug.gb10smmu :
.Bl -tag -width Ds -offset indent
.It Va dump
Writable.
Writing 1 to it prints the state of the SMMU, the facts the IORT gave and
the events the event queue holds:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
sysctl debug.gb10smmu.dump=1
.Ed
.It Va events
Read-only.
The number of events taken from the event queue:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
sysctl debug.gb10smmu.events
.Ed
.It Va mode
Read-only.
The mode the module runs in:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
sysctl debug.gb10smmu.mode
.Ed
.El
.Pp
See
.Xr sysctl 8 .
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr acpi 4 ,
.Xr loader.conf 5 ,
.Xr kldload 8 ,
.Xr sysctl 8
